<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arslantepe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arslantepe is an archaeological mound near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the site of ancient Melid. Settled from at least the fifth millennium BCE, it held a palace complex of the late fourth millennium with some of the oldest known swords. In the early first millennium BCE it was the citadel of a Neo-Hittite kingdom, whose lion gate and reliefs are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arslantepe.jpg" alt="Arslantepe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeynel Cebeci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hittite_lions,_Arslantepe_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
